Cell["\<\
This matrix has 48 rows and 29 columns. The first two columns correspond to \
the two pieces while the remaining columns correspond to the 27 unit cubes in \
a 3x3x3 puzzle as described above. The rows beginning with \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]10\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] correspond to the 12 \
possible positions that the blue piece could occupy inside an empty 3x3x3 \
cube while the rows beginning with \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]01\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] correspond to the 36 positions that the orange piece \
could occupy.

We then compute an exact cover of this matrix, which is a list of rows of the \
matrix such that each column is filled by a 1 in exactly one row of the \
solution and by 0 in the others. The number of rows in the solution will be \
equal to the number of pieces in the puzzle and each represents the location \
of a piece. \
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.944435811207304*^9, 3.944435817006649*^9}, {
   3.94443591832197*^9, 3.9444359246909275`*^9}, {3.944436185111202*^9, 
   3.9444364049312897`*^9}, {3.944436459831396*^9, 3.944436556951576*^9}, {
   3.94443696583091*^9, 3.9444372371858826`*^9}, {3.944437272825306*^9, 
   3.9444372832412453`*^9}, {3.9444380594095287`*^9, 3.944438089367735*^9}, {
